var definitions = ['<h2>Paul et Linda sont amis.</h2><div><strong>Paul</strong> This is a proper noun, a first name common in French and English. Here, it is the first subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This is the French word for "and". It’s a conjunction used to link words together, in this case, Paul and Linda.</div><div><strong>Linda</strong> A proper noun which is a first name, typically used in English, but also known in French. Here, it is the second subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It indicates the state of being of the subjects Paul and Linda.</div><div><strong>amis</strong> This is a French noun meaning "friends". It is the masculine plural form, which can be used to refer to a group of mixed or same gender in French. In this context, it tells us about the relationship between Paul and Linda.</div>','<h2>C’est abordable et de bonne qualité.</h2><div><strong>C’</strong> This is the contraction of "ce" and "est", standing for "it" and "is", respectively. "C’" prefigures "est" to form the contraction that translates to "it’s" in English. It’s a neutral gender subject pronoun and part of the verb "être" in the present tense.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", meaning "to be". In this sentence, it links the subject with the attributes "abordable" and "de bonne qualité".</div><div><strong>abordable</strong> This is a French adjective that means "affordable". It describes something that can be obtained without spending too much money.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This is the French word for "and". It is a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses, indicating that what follows is an addition or complement to what precedes it.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This French preposition is used here as part of an expression indicating quality. It translates to "of" in English.</div><div><strong>bonne</strong> This is the feminine form of the French adjective "bon", meaning "good". It is used here because "qualité" is a feminine noun. It indicates that something is of high or respectable standard.</div><div><strong>qualité</strong> This is a French noun meaning "quality". In this context, it refers to the overall standard or grade of a product or item.</div>','<h2>J’aime le vin et le fromage.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is a contraction of "Je", which is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s contracted to "J’" because the following word starts with a vowel sound, which is common in French to ease pronunciation.</div><div><strong>aime</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to love" or "to like". It indicates the subject’s affection or preference for something.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article for masculine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>vin</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French that means "wine". It’s the direct object of the verb "aime" in this sentence, indicating what the subject likes.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect words, phrases, or clauses.</div><div><strong>le</strong> (repeated) As mentioned earlier, this is the definite article for masculine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used here again to introduce another noun.</div><div><strong>fromage</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French that means "cheese". It’s another direct object of the verb "aime", connected to "vin" with the conjunction "et", indicating another item that the subject likes.</div>','<h2>Les pommes, les oranges et les bananes sont tous des fruits.</h2><div><strong>Les</strong> This is the French definite article for plural nouns, translated as "the" in English. It’s used here before each fruit to signify that we are talking about these fruits in general.</div><div><strong>pommes</strong> The French word for "apples", which is a plural noun here. Apples are one of the fruits being listed.</div><div><strong>les</strong> Again, this is the French definite article for plural nouns. It is repeated before each item in the list.</div><div><strong>oranges</strong> The French word for "oranges". Similar to "pommes", it is also a plural noun representing another type of fruit in the list.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This is the French conjunction meaning "and". It is used to connect the items in the list.</div><div><strong>les</strong> The definite article "les" is used once again for the next item in the list, to maintain the pattern and indicate that these are general terms.</div><div><strong>bananes</strong> The French word for "bananas". As with "pommes" and "oranges", "bananes" is plural because the sentence is referring to bananas in general, not a specific number.</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subjects (apples, oranges, and bananas) to the predicate (being fruits).</div><div><strong>tous</strong> The French word "tous" means "all" in English. In this sentence, it implies that all items in the list (apples, oranges, and bananas) share the characteristic of being fruits.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is the plural indefinite article in French, which translates to "some" in English, but in this context it is better understood as "are" as part of the verb "to be". It is used before "fruits" to indicate an unspecified quantity or to refer to fruits in general.</div><div><strong>fruits</strong> The French word for "fruits". It’s in the plural form and it represents the category to which apples, oranges, and bananas belong.</div>','<h2>J’ai un chat et un chien.</h2><div><strong>J’ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It’s combined with the subject pronoun "je" (I) and contracted as in spoken French, indicating possession by the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>un chat</strong> This phrase means "a cat" in English. "Un" is a masculine singular indefinite article equivalent to "a" in English, and "chat" is the French word for "cat".</div><div><strong>et</strong> This is the French word for "and". It’s a conjunction used to connect words or groups of words.</div><div><strong>un chien</strong> This phrase translates to "a dog" in English. Similar to "un chat", "un" is a masculine singular indefinite article, and "chien" means "dog".</div>','<h2>Préfères-tu les fruits ou les légumes?</h2><div><strong>Préfères-tu</strong> This is a contraction of "préfères" (you prefer) and "tu" (you), forming an inversion which is common in French for asking a question. It is the second-person singular present tense of the verb "préférer", meaning "to prefer".</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French for both masculine and feminine plural nouns, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>fruits</strong> This is a masculine plural noun meaning "fruits" in English.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This is a conjunction meaning "or" in English, used to offer a choice between two options.</div><div><strong>les légumes?</strong> Similar to the previous "les", this definite article is followed by "légumes", a masculine plural noun meaning "vegetables" in English. The question mark indicates that the sentence is a question.</div>','<h2>Veux-tu aller au cinéma ou au restaurant?</h2><div><strong>Veux-tu</strong> This is a combination of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want", and the second-person singular pronoun "tu", equivalent to "you" in English. It forms the question, "Do you want", in the informal tone.</div><div><strong>aller</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb which means "to go" in English.</div><div><strong>au</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "à" and the definite article "le", which together translate to "to the" when followed by a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>cinéma</strong> This is a masculine singular noun meaning "cinema" or "movie theater" in English.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This word translates to "or" in English. It’s a conjunction used to offer a choice between two or more alternatives.</div><div><strong>au restaurant?</strong> Similar to "au cinéma", this phrase combines "au" (to the) with "restaurant", which means the same as in English, offering an alternative place to go.</div>','<h2>Elle doit choisir de prendre la pomme ou le gâteau.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>doit</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "must" or "has to". It indicates an obligation or a necessity.</div><div><strong>choisir</strong> This is an infinitive verb meaning "to choose". It follows the verb "devoir" in this sentence to form the phrase "must choose" or "has to choose".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This preposition is used here as part of the infinitive phrase "de prendre", which means "to take". It can also mean "of" or "from" in other contexts.</div><div><strong>prendre</strong> This is another infinitive verb meaning "to take". It’s used following the preposition "de" to indicate the action of taking.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article for feminine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>pomme</strong> This means "apple" in English. It is a feminine singular noun and is the first option offered in this sentence.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This is the French word for "or". It is used as a conjunction to offer a choice between two options.</div><div><strong>le</strong> Similar to "la", this is the definite article for masculine singular nouns in French, translating to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>gâteau</strong> This means "cake" in English. It is a masculine singular noun and is the second option presented in the sentence.</div>','<h2>Veux-tu l’horloge noire ou la blanche?</h2><div><strong>Veux-tu</strong> This is a combination of "veux", which is the second-person singular present tense of the verb "vouloir", meaning "to want", and the pronoun "tu", meaning "you" in English. Together, "Veux-tu" forms a question that translates to "Do you want".</div><div><strong>l’horloge</strong> This is the French word for "the clock". "L’" is the elided form of the definite article "la" used before a noun starting with a vowel or a mute "h" to ease pronunciation.</div><div><strong>noire</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "black". It agrees in gender and number with "l’horloge", which is feminine singular.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This is the French word for "or". It’s a conjunction used to offer a choice between two options.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the feminine singular definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used here before a feminine noun.</div><div><strong>blanche</strong> This is another French adjective meaning "white". Similar to "noire", it also agrees in gender and number with "l’horloge", which is feminine singular.</div><div>The sentence as a whole, "Veux-tu l’horloge noire ou la blanche?" translates to "Do you want the black clock or the white one?"</div>','<h2>Ma main droite ou ma main gauche?</h2><div><strong>Ma</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, which means "my" in English. It indicates that the following noun belongs to the speaker.</div><div><strong>main</strong> This is a noun in French, meaning "hand". It’s feminine and singular, and is used here to denote one of the hands of the speaker.</div><div><strong>droite</strong> This is an adjective in French, which means "right" in English. It’s used here to specify which hand the speaker is referring to.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This word translates to "or" in English. It’s a conjunction used to offer a choice between two alternatives.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> Again, this is the possessive adjective "my" in English, indicating the speaker’s ownership or association with the next noun.</div><div><strong>main</strong> The noun repeats here, again meaning "hand" in English, now referring to the other hand of the speaker.</div><div><strong>gauche?</strong> This is an adjective in French, which means "left" in English. It specifies the other hand that could be chosen as an alternative to the right hand.</div>','<h2>Il a aussi beaucoup de talent.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It indicates possession, existence, or a characteristic related to the subject.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "also" or "as well". It indicates that what is being said applies in addition to other statements or facts mentioned.</div><div><strong>beaucoup de</strong> This phrase translates to "a lot of" in English. It is used to express a large quantity or degree of something.</div><div><strong>talent</strong> This is a French noun meaning "talent". It refers to a natural aptitude or skill.</div>','<h2>Elle court souvent dehors aussi.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>court</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "courir", which means "to run". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>souvent</strong> This is an adverb meaning "often". It describes the frequency at which the action of the verb occurs.</div><div><strong>dehors</strong> This is an adverb meaning "outside". It indicates the location where the action of the verb is taking place.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This word translates to "also" in English. It’s an adverb used to add information that is similar to or in addition to already mentioned details.</div>','<h2>Moi aussi, je t’aime!</h2><div><strong>Moi aussi</strong> This phrase means "me too" or "I also" in English. "Moi" is a stressed pronoun for "me" and "aussi" means "too" or "also". The phrase indicates agreement or shared feeling with a preceding statement, often used in response to someone expressing a sentiment or emotion.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. In this sentence, "je" expresses the subject which is the person speaking.</div><div><strong>t’aime</strong> This is a contraction of "te" (the second-person singular object pronoun equivalent to "you" in English) and "aime" (the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to love"). Together, "t’aime" translates to "love you" in English, indicating that the speaker is expressing their love for the person addressed.</div>','<h2>Elle doit répondre à son autre appel aussi.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>doit</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must". It expresses an obligation or necessity.</div><div><strong>répondre</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb which means "to answer" or "to respond". It is used here in conjunction with the previous verb "doit", forming the phrase "must answer".</div><div><strong>à son</strong> This phrase combines the preposition "à" (to) with the possessive adjective "son" (his/her), referring to something that belongs to a previously mentioned or implied "he" or "she".</div><div><strong>autre</strong> This is the French adjective meaning "other" or "another". It specifies that the call she must respond to is an additional one.</div><div><strong>appel</strong> This is the French noun for "call". In this context, it most likely refers to a telephone call.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This is an adverb meaning "also" or "as well" in English. It indicates that the action of responding to the call is an additional action to something else implied or previously mentioned.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>Oui, et où est-ce qu’on se rencontre?</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It is the affirmative response to a question or statement.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses.</div><div><strong>où</strong> This is the French word for "where". It is used to ask about a location or a place.</div><div><strong>est-ce</strong> This phrase is a part of a fixed expression "est-ce que", which is used to form questions in French. It is a formal way to ask a question, equivalent to the English construction "is it that".</div><div><strong>qu’on</strong> This is a contraction of "que" (which or that) and "on" (one, we, people, or someone). In this context, it’s equivalent to "we" in English, representing the subject of the verb in the question.</div><div><strong>se rencontre</strong> This is the reflexive form of the verb "rencontrer", which means "to meet". In this question, it means "we meet" or "meeting each other". The reflexive pronoun "se" indicates that the action of the verb is being done by the subject to itself.</div>','<h2>As-tu des frères et soeurs?</h2><div><strong>As-tu</strong> This phrase is a contraction of "as" (second-person singular present tense form of "avoir", which means "to have") and "tu" (second-person singular pronoun, equivalent to "you" in English). It forms a question and translates to "do you have" in English.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is an indefinite plural article in French, which can often be translated as "some" in English. Here, it precedes a plural noun without specifying the quantity.</div><div><strong>frères</strong> This is the French word for "brothers". It is a masculine plural noun, referring to male siblings.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to link words together.</div><div><strong>soeurs?</strong> This is the French word for "sisters". It is a feminine plural noun, referring to female siblings. The question mark indicates that this is an interrogative sentence, asking if the person has any siblings.</div>','<h2>Oui, je joue de la guitare et du piano.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It’s an affirmative response to a question or statement.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>joue</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "jouer", which means "to play". It describes the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>de la</strong> This phrase, which means "of the" or "from the" in English, is used before feminine singular nouns in French to indicate playing instruments or other activities involving instruments or materials.</div><div><strong>guitare</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French meaning "guitar".</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses within a sentence.</div><div><strong>du</strong> This phrase is a contraction of "de" (of/from) and "le" in French, creating "du". It is used before masculine singular nouns when talking about playing instruments or other activities involving instruments or materials.</div><div><strong>piano</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French meaning "piano".</div>','<h2>D’accord, et voulez-vous un dessert?</h2><div><strong>D’accord</strong> This is a French expression meaning "okay" or "alright". It is an acknowledgment or an agreement to what has been said before.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It is a conjunction used to connect words, phrases, or clauses in a sentence.</div><div><strong>voulez-vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural form of the verb "vouloir", meaning "to want". In the polite form, it is also used to address someone formally in the second-person singular. It means "do you want" in English, used here in a question.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is a French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, which translates to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>dessert</strong> This noun is the same in both French and English. It refers to the sweet course typically served at the end of a meal.</div>','<h2>Oui, je joue au tennis et au football.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It affirms a statement or responds positively to a question.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>joue</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "jouer", which means "to play". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>au</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "à" and the definite article "le", translating to "to the" or "at the" in English. In the context of sports, it means "play" a particular sport.</div><div><strong>tennis</strong> This is a noun in French, identical to the English word "tennis", referring to the sport played on a rectangular court with a net running across the middle.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect words, phrases, or clauses.</div><div><strong>au football</strong> The phrase "au football" combines the preposition-article contraction "au" with the noun "football", which is the French word for "football" (referred to as "soccer" in the United States).</div>','<h2>Ah bon. Et où voulez-vous aller?</h2><div><strong>Ah bon</strong> This expression can mean "Really? Is that so?" or "I see" in English. It indicates surprise, realization, or seeking confirmation. The speaker is acknowledging what was previously said or asking for more information.</div><div><strong>Et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses.</div><div><strong>où</strong> This is the French word for "where". It is used to ask about location or direction.</div><div><strong>voulez-vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural form of "vouloir", which means "to want". The word "vous" can mean "you" in both singular formal and plural forms. In this phrase, "voulez-vous" is part of a common question structure.</div><div><strong>aller</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to go" in French. It’s being used in this question to ask about the person’s desired destination.</div>','<h2>Vous avez le siège vingt A et le vol cent-deux.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s the formal way of addressing an individual, or it can refer to multiple people. In this sentence, it’s the subject.</div><div><strong>avez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural or singular form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It indicates possession or attribute to the subject.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes "siège" to denote a specific seat.</div><div><strong>siège</strong> This is a noun meaning "seat". In the context of this sentence, it refers to a specific seat assignment, such as on a plane or a train.</div><div><strong>vingt A</strong> This is a seat identifier typically used on transportation, such as aircraft or trains, indicating the seat is in row twenty, seat "A".</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses.</div><div><strong>le vol</strong> Here, "le" is again the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, and "vol" means "flight". Together "le vol" translates to "the flight" in English.</div><div><strong>cent-deux</strong> This is a number in French, meaning "one hundred two". It typically would be a flight number in this context.</div>','<h2>J’ai lu un livre et j’ai beaucoup dormi.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contraction of "je", the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English, with the following verb that starts with a vowel or a mute h. It’s used as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it’s used to form the past compound tense, known as "le passé composé".</div><div><strong>lu</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "lire", which means "to read". It’s used here with the auxiliary verb "ai" to indicate the past action of reading.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, which translates as "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>livre</strong> This is a masculine singular noun meaning "book" in English.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses.</div><div><strong>j’</strong> Again, this is the contraction of "je", as the subject of the next clause.</div><div><strong>ai</strong> As before, this is the first-person singular present tense form of "avoir", used as the auxiliary in the passé composé.</div><div><strong>beaucoup</strong> This is an adverb meaning "a lot" or "much" in English, indicating the extent of the action that follows.</div><div><strong>dormi</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "dormir", which means "to sleep". It’s combined with the auxiliary verb "ai" to convey the past action of sleeping, with intensity expressed through "beaucoup".</div>','<h2>J’ai du mal à respirer et je tousse.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contraction of "je" (I) with the following word beginning with a vowel or mute h, in this case "ai". It is used to make the pronunciation easier.</div><div><strong>ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this context, it’s used to indicate possession of difficulty.</div><div><strong>du mal</strong> This phrase translates to "difficulty" in English. "Du" is a partitive article in this case, and "mal" means pain or difficulty.</div><div><strong>à respirer</strong> This phrase means "to breathe". "À" is a preposition that is often used to link verbs to their infinitive form; "respirer" is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to breathe".</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the clause here.</div><div><strong>tousse</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "tousser", which means "to cough". It describes an action the subject is currently performing.</div>','<h2>Je veux voyager encore et encore.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want" or "to wish". It expresses a desire or intention.</div><div><strong>voyager</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "voyager", which means "to travel". In this sentence, it functions as the main verb, indicating the action that the subject wants to do.</div><div><strong>encore</strong> This is an adverb meaning "again" or "still" in English. In this context, it indicates a repetition or continuation of the action.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used here to connect the two occurrences of "encore" for emphasis.</div><div><strong>encore</strong> Repeated for emphasis, it underscores the intensity or diligence of the subject’s desire to travel.</div>','<h2>J’aime te voir sourire et t’amuser.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contraction of "Je", which is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. The contraction is used before a vowel sound to ease pronunciation.</div><div><strong>aime</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to love" or "to like". Here, it indicates what the subject of the sentence enjoys doing.</div><div><strong>te</strong> This is the second-person singular object pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used here as the indirect object of the verb, referring to the person being loved or liked.</div><div><strong>voir</strong> This is the infinitive form of the French verb meaning "to see". In this sentence, it is used after the verb "aime" to express what the speaker likes, which is "to see you".</div><div><strong>sourire</strong> This is the infinitive form of the French verb meaning "to smile". It’s the action the speaker likes seeing the person perform.</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses within the sentence.</div><div><strong>t’amuser</strong> This is a reflexive verb in the infinitive form composed of "te" (contracting to "t’" before a vowel sound) meaning "yourself" and "amuser" meaning "to have fun" or "to enjoy oneself". Here, it’s the second action that the speaker enjoys seeing the person do.</div>','<h2>Je serai payée à la semaine ou au mois?</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>serai</strong> This is the future tense of the first-person singular form of the verb "être" (to be), which means "will be" in English. It is used here to indicate a future action or state.</div><div><strong>payée</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the past participle of the verb "payer" (to pay), used here in agreement with the subject, who is presumed to be female. It indicates the action that will happen to the subject in the future ("will be paid"). If the subject were male, it would be "payé".</div><div><strong>à la</strong> This phrase is a combination of the preposition "à" (to) and the definite article "la" (the), used here to denote a manner or frequency of payment and it means "by the" or "per" in this context.</div><div><strong>semaine</strong> This is a French noun meaning "week". In this sentence, it is the unit of time used to describe the frequency of payment.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This is the French word for "or". It presents an alternative between two options.</div><div><strong>au</strong> This phrase is a contraction of the preposition "à" and the definite article "le", resulting in "au" which means "to the" or "per". Here, it precedes "mois" to say "by the month".</div><div><strong>mois?</strong> This is a French noun meaning "month". In this context, it is the second option for the frequency of payment being queried.</div>','<h2>Dans votre compte-chèques ou épargne?</h2><div><strong>Dans</strong> This is a French preposition that means "in" or "into" in English. It indicates the location or the context within which something takes place.</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, which translates as "your" in English. It denotes possession or association with the person addressed, and agrees with the noun it modifies in number and gender.</div><div><strong>compte-chèques</strong> This is a compound noun in French, meaning "checking account" in English. It refers to a bank account from which money can be withdrawn without any need to notify the bank in advance.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This is the French word for "or". It’s a conjunction used to offer alternatives or choices between two or more items.</div><div><strong>épargne</strong> This is a French noun meaning "savings" in English. It refers to a savings account or the act of saving money. It’s singular and often used to denote a type of bank account intended for saving money over a long period.</div>','<h2>Est-ce que c’est une voiture neuve ou d’occasion?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce que</strong> This is a French phrase used to introduce a yes-or-no question. It can be literally translated as "Is it that" but is more naturally expressed in English as simply "Is" or "Does" at the beginning of a question.</div><div><strong>c’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (it/this/that) and "est" (is), the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". Thus, "c’est" can be translated as "it is" or "this is" or "that is" depending on the context.</div><div><strong>une voiture</strong> This phrase means "a car" in English. "Une" is a feminine indefinite article, and "voiture" is a feminine noun that means "car".</div><div><strong>neuve</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "new". It’s used in the feminine singular form here to agree with "une voiture".</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This is the French word for "or". It is a conjunction used to offer a choice between two or more items or ideas.</div><div><strong>d’occasion</strong> This is a phrase meaning "secondhand" or "used" in English. It’s a contraction of "de" (of/from) and "occasion" (chance/opportunity), but in this context, it translates to "used" when talking about items like cars or other goods.</div>','<h2>Préfères-tu aller à droite ou à gauche?</h2><div><strong>Préfères-tu</strong> This is a combination of the verb "préférer" (to prefer) in the present tense second-person singular, and the subject pronoun "tu" (you), forming a question. Inverting the pronoun and verb (adding a hyphen) is a common way to ask a question in formal written French.</div><div><strong>aller</strong> This is an infinitive verb meaning "to go". In the sentence, it’s being used as the main verb following "préfères-tu", to indicate the action being preferred.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a preposition that can mean "to", "at", or "in" depending on context. Here it indicates direction "to the right" or "to the left".</div><div><strong>droite</strong> This is a noun meaning "right", referring to the direction.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This word translates to "or" in English. It’s a conjunction used to offer a choice or alternative.</div><div><strong>à gauche?</strong> Similarly to "à droite", this combination means "to the left", with "gauche" being the noun that means "left" for direction. The question mark indicates that the sentence is a question.</div>','<h2>J’aimerais bien faire du shopping ou regarder un film.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is a contraction of "je" (I) with the subsequent verb that begins with a vowel sound, which is common in French. The contraction is used to improve the flow of the sentence.</div><div><strong>aimerais</strong> This verb is the conditional tense of "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love". The conditional tense is used here to express a polite desire or preference, equivalent to "would like" in English.</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This adverb translates to "well" or "really" in English and is used here for emphasis to strengthen the statement of preference.</div><div><strong>faire</strong> This verb means "to do" or "to make". In this context, "faire du shopping" translates to "go shopping" or "do shopping".</div><div><strong>du</strong> This is the contraction of "de" (of/from) and "le" (the), forming a partitive article that is used to refer to an unspecified quantity of something. Here, "du" references shopping in a general sense.</div><div><strong>shopping</strong> This borrowing from English refers to the activity of purchasing goods or going to shops.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This word means "or" in English, and it’s used to give an alternative or choice.</div><div><strong>regarder</strong> This verb translates to "to watch" or "to look at". In this sentence, it pertains to watching a film.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This indefinite article means "a" or "an" in English. It is used before masculine singular nouns.</div><div><strong>film.</strong> This noun means "movie" or "film" in English.</div>','<h2>Je ne sais pas si je veux porter celui-ci ou celui-là.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne ... pas</strong> This is the negation structure in French, equivalent to "not" in English. It surrounds the verb to make the statement negative.</div><div><strong>sais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "savoir", which means "to know". It indicates the speaker’s knowledge or state of being aware.</div><div><strong>si</strong> This word translates to "if" in English. It is a conjunction used to introduce a conditional phrase or an indirect question.</div><div><strong>je veux</strong> This phrase combines the first-person singular pronoun "je" with the present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It expresses the speaker’s desire or will.</div><div><strong>porter</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to wear" in English. It is used after the verb "vouloir" to indicate what the speaker wants to do.</div><div><strong>celui-ci</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French which means "this one" in English. It is referring to a nearby object or one that has just been mentioned.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This word translates to "or" in English. It is a conjunction used to present alternatives or choices.</div><div><strong>celui-là</strong> This is another demonstrative pronoun in French which means "that one" in English. It usually refers to an object that is farther away or has been mentioned previously in contrast to "celui-ci".</div>','<h2>Vous pourriez aussi avoir la nausée.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It can indicate formality, plural, or both, depending on the context. It’s the subject of the clause.</div><div><strong>pourriez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural form of the conditional tense of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can" in English. It indicates possibility or capability.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This is an adverb in French that means "also" or "too" in English. It adds information about an additional possibility or action.</div><div><strong>avoir</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French that translates to "to have" in English. It is used here after the modal verb "pourriez" to express what can possibly be had or experienced.</div><div><strong>la nausée</strong> This is a French noun phrase meaning "nausea" in English. "La" is the definite article "the", and "nausée" is a feminine singular noun meaning the feeling of sickness with an inclination to vomit.</div>','<h2>Oui, j’étudie aussi le français avec Ouino.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It is an affirmation in response to a statement or question.</div><div><strong>j’étudie</strong> This is a conjugation of the verb "étudier", which means "to study". "j’étudie" is the first-person singular present tense, meaning "I study".</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This translates to "also" or "as well" in English. It’s an adverb used to add information to the verb, indicating that the action is in addition to another action.</div><div><strong>le français</strong> This means "the French language" in English. "le" is the definite article "the", and "français" is the noun "French" referring to the language.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This means "with" in English. It’s a preposition used here to indicate accompaniment or association.</div><div><strong>Ouino</strong> This is likely a proper noun, the name of a language learning software or program. In this context, it is not translated and is used to specify the method with which the speaker is studying French.</div>','<h2>Une seule, mais j’ai aussi un bagage à main.</h2><div><strong>Une</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the French indefinite article, equivalent to "a" or "one" in English. It’s used here to indicate a single item.</div><div><strong>seule</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "only" or "single". In this sentence, it complements the indefinite article "une" to emphasize that there is only one of something.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word means "but" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a phrase or clause contrasting with what has been mentioned previously.</div><div><strong>j’</strong> This is the contraction of "je" (the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English) and the vowel of the following verb, which starts with a vowel here, to ease pronunciation. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have" in English. It indicates possession or presence.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This means "also" in English. It’s used here to add additional information to the statement.</div><div><strong>un bagage</strong> This is a masculine singular noun phrase in French, meaning "a bag" or "a piece of luggage" in English.</div><div><strong>à main</strong> This prepositional phrase means "hand" in English (literally "to hand") and describes the type of bag or luggage being mentioned—specifically, a hand luggage or carry-on.</div>','<h2>Oui. J’aimerais aussi avoir une télévision.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It is used to affirm a statement or agree with a question.</div><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contraction of "je", the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English, before a vowel or mute h. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>aimerais</strong> This is the first-person singular conditional form of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love". In this tense, it conveys a wish or desire, equivalent to "would like" in English.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "also" or "as well". It is used to add information to the sentence.</div><div><strong>avoir</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French which means "to have". It’s used here to express the desire to possess something.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is a French indefinite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>télévision</strong> This is a French noun meaning "television". It is feminine and singular to match the article "une".</div>','<h2>Vous devez aussi éviter de boire de l’alcool.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. In this context, it may be used in a formal address or to refer to more than one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>devez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural present tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must". It indicates an obligation or necessity.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This word translates to "also" in English. It’s an adverb used to add additional information to the verb or clause.</div><div><strong>éviter</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to avoid". In this sentence, it is paired with the verb "devez" to indicate the action that must be taken.</div><div><strong>de</strong> Here, "de" is used in the construction "éviter de" which means "to avoid [doing something]". It precedes the verb indicating the action to avoid.</div><div><strong>boire</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to drink". It is the action to be avoided in the sentence.</div><div><strong>de l’alcool</strong> The phrase "de l’alcool" translates to "alcohol" in English. "L’alcool" is a masculine singular noun meaning "alcohol", and the preceding "de" can be translated as "some" or "any" in this context, indicating an undefined quantity of alcohol to avoid.</div>','<h2>Je vais aussi vous faire faire une prise de sang.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it is used to form the near future tense, expressing a forthcoming action.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This is the French adverb meaning "also" or "too". It adds additional information indicating that the action is in addition to something else.</div><div><strong>vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It serves as the indirect object in this sentence, meaning that the action is being done to the person or people being spoken to. It can also imply formality or politeness.</div><div><strong>faire</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to do" or "to make". In this sentence, it is used in conjunction with "vous", forming a causative construction that means "to have you do" or "to make you do".</div><div><strong>faire</strong> This infinitive is repeated as a part of the causative construction mentioned before. Here, it’s connected with "une prise de sang" and it means that someone is going to be made to have a blood test.</div><div><strong>une prise de sang</strong> This phrase literally translates to "a taking of blood", which refers to a blood test or blood draw in medical contexts.</div>','<h2>J’ai aussi entendu dire que vous avez une piscine.</h2><div><strong>J’ai</strong> This is a contraction of "Je" and "ai", where "Je" is the first-person singular pronoun "I", and "ai" is the present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". This contraction is needed because two vowels cannot be next to each other. Here, it’s used as part of a complex verbal structure.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "also" or "too". It’s used to add information that is similar to what has already been mentioned.</div><div><strong>entendu</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "entendre", which means "to hear". It is used here in a compound tense with "ai" to form the past tense, indicating a completed action of having heard something.</div><div><strong>dire</strong> This is an infinitive verb meaning "to say" or "to tell". In this structure, it implies reported speech, and it follows "entendu" to indicate what was heard.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction which translates to "that" in English. It introduces a dependent clause, translating the content of what was heard.</div><div><strong>vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It can be used for formal address or to refer to more than one person. Here, it’s the subject of the dependent clause.</div><div><strong>avez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". This verb is used with "vous" to indicate possession.</div><div><strong>une piscine</strong> This means "a swimming pool" in English. "Une" is the indefinite article for feminine nouns, and "piscine" is the feminine noun for "pool".</div>','<h2>Génial! Je veux y aller aussi.</h2><div><strong>Génial!</strong> This is a French exclamation meaning "Awesome!" or "Great!". It’s an adjective used here as an interjection to express enthusiasm or admiration.</div><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates a desire or intention.</div><div><strong>y</strong> This is a French pronoun that generally means "there". It is used with the verb "aller" to indicate motion towards a place already mentioned or known in the conversation. It replaces phrases that begin with the prepositions "à", "chez", "dans", etc.</div><div><strong>aller</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French that means "to go". It indicates the action of moving or traveling to a destination.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This is an adverb meaning "also" or "too" in English. It’s used here to indicate that the speaker has the same desire or intention as someone else.</div>','<h2>Moi aussi, j’ai passé une très bonne soirée.</h2><div><strong>Moi aussi</strong> This phrase in French means "me too" or "I also". It is an agreement or addition to a statement made by someone else, indicating that the speaker shares the same experience or sentiment.</div><div><strong>j’ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir" in the passé composé tense. In English, it translates to "I have". The passé composé is a past tense that indicates an action that has been completed.</div><div><strong>passé</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "passer", which generally means "to pass" but it can also mean "to spend" when talking about time. In this context, it means "spent", as in time spent during an event or activity.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the indefinite article in French, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It precedes a feminine noun.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is an adverb meaning "very" in English. It is used to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>bonne</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the adjective "bon", which means "good" in English. In this sentence, it describes the quality of the evening, matching the feminine form of the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>soirée</strong> This is a feminine noun meaning "evening" in English. In this context, it denotes the time spent during the night, typically in a social context.</div>','<h2>La carte postale aussi?</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the French definite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun it is referring to.</div><div><strong>carte postale</strong> This is a compound noun in French, meaning "postcard" in English. "Carte" is a feminine noun meaning "card", and "postale" is an adjective that relates to "post" or "mail", together referring to the mail-ready card.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This means "also" or "as well" in English. It’s an adverb and is often placed at the end of a question or statement to inquire or state if something applies in addition to what has already been mentioned.</div>','<h2>Et vous passez la soie dentaire aussi?</h2><div><strong>Et</strong> This is the French word for "and". It’s often used to connect sentences or concepts together.</div><div><strong>vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used as the subject of a verb, or as a polite form of address.</div><div><strong>passez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural form of the verb "passer", which generally means "to pass". In this context, it is used reflexively ("vous passez") to mean "you pass" or "do" in reference to an action directed towards oneself.</div><div><strong>la soie dentaire</strong> This phrase translates to "the dental floss" in English. "La" is the feminine singular definite article ("the"), indicating a specific item, "soie" means "silk" but in this context refers to "floss", and "dentaire" means "dental". Together, "la soie dentaire" refers to the act of using dental floss.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This word means "also" or "too" in English. It’s used here at the end of the question to ask if the action (using dental floss) is also performed by the person being addressed.</div>','<h2>Veuillez vider vos poches aussi.</h2><div><strong>Veuillez</strong> This is the formal French word for "please", used when making a request or a command. It comes from the verb "vouloir", which means "to want", in the imperative form and inflected for politeness.</div><div><strong>vider</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb that means "to empty". In this sentence, it is part of a command phrase started by "Veuillez".</div><div><strong>vos</strong> This is the possessive adjective meaning "your" in French. It is the plural form and denotes that "poches" belong to the person being spoken to. It can address either one person in a formal context or several people.</div><div><strong>poches</strong> This noun translates to "pockets". The word is plural, which is why "vos" is used instead of "votre" (the singular form for "your").</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This is the French word for "also" or "as well". It is being used here to possibly emphasize that the action should be done in addition to something else that was said or implied before.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);